// src/widgets/text_input.pdx -- postui.M4-001 (paideia-os/postui#27)
//
// TextInput / TextArea widget: cursor math, byte-level insert/delete,
// UTF-8 codepoint-aware cursor movement, single-line or wrapped
// multi-line render, plus TextInputView@0.1 semantic-pipe record.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE (docs/design.md §2.6 widget catalog row TextInput; §5.1 M4-001)
// =============================================================================
//
// TextInput is the 14th ordinal (WK_TEXTINPUT = 13) in the closed widget
// catalog and the first M4 landing. It is the first postui widget with
// mutating state: unlike Paragraph / List / Table (all read-only over a
// caller-supplied Text or Row array), TextInput OWNS a caller-provided
// UTF-8 byte buffer through spec fields (buffer_ptr, buffer_cap,
// buffer_len, cursor_byte) and mutates it in-place via the insert /
// delete / cursor_left / cursor_right primitives. The buffer itself is
// still caller-owned memory (no heap allocation, matching postui's
// no-allocator posture) -- TextInput only bumps the len / cursor
// bookkeeping fields inside the spec and shifts bytes inside the
// caller's buffer.
//
// This landing ships:
//   - The TextInputSpec state record (72-byte struct laid out in memory;
//     see WIRE block below). One packed Rect + eight u64 fields.
//   - text_input_spec_init: pack a TextInputSpec into caller-provided
//     memory. Included for parity with every other widget's spec_init
//     even though the M4-001 issue text does not name it explicitly --
//     downstream tests and callers need a total constructor that does
//     not skip the discipline of writing every slot.
//   - text_input_insert: insert one byte at cursor, advance cursor,
//     shift tail. Returns TI_ERR_BUFFER_FULL if the buffer has no room.
//   - text_input_delete: backspace -- delete byte before cursor, shift
//     tail left. Returns TI_ERR_CURSOR_AT_START if cursor == 0.
//   - text_input_cursor_left: walk cursor back one UTF-8 codepoint,
//     respecting continuation bytes so the cursor never lands mid-
//     codepoint.
//   - text_input_cursor_right: walk cursor forward one UTF-8 codepoint,
//     using the lead-byte class (2/3/4-byte) to compute the advance.
//   - text_input_draw: widget_draw-shape entry (spec_ptr, buf_ptr, rows,
//     cols) -> u64. SingleLine mode renders one visible row honouring
//     scroll_col; MultiLine mode word-wraps to the Rect width via
//     Text::line_break_positions and stamps into successive rows. Both
//     modes overlay a cursor cell (style_cursor) at the byte address
//     cursor_byte -- or, when the cursor sits past the last byte
//     (cursor_byte == buffer_len), at the empty display cell where the
//     next inserted byte would appear.
//   - text_input_view_pack: pack a TextInputView@0.1 semantic-pipe
//     record (40 bytes; magic "TIVW", record_len, rect, cursor_byte,
//     buffer_len, mode) into caller-provided memory. The buffer BYTES
//     themselves are deliberately NOT copied through the semantic pipe
//     -- per BlockView / ParagraphView precedent the view is a control-
//     plane snapshot, and text bytes are per-process behind buffer_ptr.
//
// =============================================================================
// TEXTINPUTSPEC WIRE (72 bytes, TI_SPEC_BYTES)
// =============================================================================
//
//   [+ 0..+ 8)   rect           u64 packed (x, y, w, h) per Rect wire
//                                    (identical to every other widget's
//                                    +0 slot; a shared cell-index helper
//                                    can be lifted later without
//                                    re-shaping this record).
//   [+ 8..+16)   buffer_ptr     u64 pointer to caller-owned UTF-8 byte
//                                    buffer. The buffer is per-process
//                                    memory (stack slot, static, or
//                                    Buffer-adjacent) -- TextInput does
//                                    NOT allocate. A null buffer_ptr is
//                                    a caller bug (insert/delete/draw
//                                    would fault on first dereference);
//                                    the constructor is total and stores
//                                    the field verbatim.
//   [+16..+24)   buffer_cap     u64 maximum bytes the buffer holds.
//                                    text_input_insert returns
//                                    TI_ERR_BUFFER_FULL when
//                                    buffer_len >= buffer_cap.
//   [+24..+32)   buffer_len     u64 current used bytes (0..=buffer_cap).
//                                    Mutated in place by insert (bump +1)
//                                    and delete (bump -1).
//   [+32..+40)   cursor_byte    u64 cursor byte offset in [0, buffer_len].
//                                    cursor_byte == buffer_len is the
//                                    "cursor at end" state; a valid
//                                    cursor is always at a codepoint
//                                    boundary (never on a UTF-8
//                                    continuation byte) because
//                                    cursor_left / cursor_right walk in
//                                    codepoint steps and insert /
//                                    delete are byte-level (the caller
//                                    is expected to feed insert / delete
//                                    sequences that preserve UTF-8
//                                    well-formedness).
//   [+40..+48)   scroll_col     u64 leftmost visible byte offset for
//                                    horizontal scroll (SingleLine mode
//                                    only). A byte at buffer offset i
//                                    lands at visible column (i -
//                                    scroll_col) when
//                                    scroll_col <= i < scroll_col + bw;
//                                    else off-screen. Ignored in
//                                    MultiLine mode (wrap consumes the
//                                    horizontal axis).
//   [+48..+56)   mode           u64 0 = SingleLine, 1 = MultiLine
//                                    (TextArea). Any other value at
//                                    draw time is treated as SingleLine
//                                    (documented v1 tolerance; a future
//                                    revision may trap through an
//                                    added TI_ERR_BAD_MODE sentinel).
//   [+56..+64)   style_normal   u64 packed Style (Style module wire) for
//                                    every non-cursor cell.
//   [+64..+72)   style_cursor   u64 packed Style for the cursor cell;
//                                    typically a REVERSED-mods variant
//                                    of style_normal (an inverse-video
//                                    block cursor). Applied to the
//                                    display cell containing cursor_byte
//                                    -- or to an empty space cell when
//                                    cursor_byte == buffer_len.
//
// The 72-byte size falls between BlockSpec/ParagraphSpec (48 B) and
// ScrollbarSpec (56 B); every field is 8-byte aligned so the draw body
// loads each with a single qword mov. 72 % 16 = 8 -- not cache-line
// aligned but no widget hot path crosses that line: text_input_draw
// reads every spec field ONCE at prologue into stack slots and never
// re-reads through spec_ptr in the render loops.
//
// The M4-001 issue estimated "~80B"; 72 B is the actual size with the
// naturally-shaped 9-slot layout. The 8-byte delta is not material to
// downstream slot-arena sizing (which already keys off the max spec
// size across kinds; TextInputSpec becomes the new max at M4 landing,
// bumping past ScrollbarSpec's 56 B).
//
// =============================================================================
// TEXTINPUTVIEW@0.1 WIRE (40 bytes fixed, TIVW_RECORD_BYTES)
// =============================================================================
//
//   [+ 0..+ 4)   magic          u32 = 0x57564954 ("TIVW" LE)
//   [+ 4..+ 8)   record_len     u32 = 40
//   [+ 8..+16)   rect_pack      u64 verbatim from TextInputSpec+0
//   [+16..+24)   cursor_byte    u64 verbatim from TextInputSpec+32
//   [+24..+32)   buffer_len     u64 verbatim from TextInputSpec+24
//   [+32..+40)   mode           u64 verbatim from TextInputSpec+48
//
// buffer_ptr / buffer_cap / scroll_col / style_normal / style_cursor
// are deliberately excluded -- buffer_ptr is a per-process address
// (semantically meaningless to a downstream reader), buffer_cap is a
// caller-allocation detail, scroll_col is transient render state, and
// the two style words are visual attributes (no semantic weight, same
// exclusion Scrollbar's own view record makes for its style_track /
// style_thumb; see src/widgets/scrollbar.pdx L142-145).
//
// The four u64 payload slots keep the record fully qword-aligned; no
// pad loop is needed. record_len at 40 (u32) matches the fixed-length
// posture ParagraphView, GaugeView, and ScrollbarView all take.
//
// TIVW_MAGIC = 0x57564954 (< 0x7FFFFFFF) so paideia-as emits the
// compact 5-byte `mov r32, imm32` form at the header store, not the
// 10-byte movabs.
//
// =============================================================================
// ERROR BAND (0xFFFFEBE0..0xFFFFEBEF, M4 batch)
// =============================================================================
//
// The M2 batch owns 0xFFFFEBC0..CF (Layout / Width / Text / Paragraph
// / Scrollbar); the M3 batch reserves 0xFFFFEBD0..DF (Gauge / Sparkline
// / BarChart / Chart / Canvas). The M4 batch starts at 0xFFFFEBE0.
// TextInput takes the low two slots:
//
//   TI_ERR_BUFFER_FULL     : 0xFFFFEBE0 -- text_input_insert with
//                            buffer_len >= buffer_cap. The insert
//                            leaves the buffer unchanged (no
//                            partial-write); the caller can free space
//                            (e.g. via text_input_delete) and retry.
//   TI_ERR_CURSOR_AT_START : 0xFFFFEBE1 -- text_input_delete with
//                            cursor_byte == 0. The delete leaves the
//                            buffer unchanged. Callers can key on this
//                            specific sentinel to render a bell / no-op
//                            audible cue rather than aborting the frame.
//
// (0xFFFFEBE2..EF reserved for future M4 widget landings: paragraph-
// scroll wraparound, list-search-not-found, table-column-overflow, etc.)
//
// Both sentinels exceed 0x7FFFFFFF -- `mov reg, imm` is emitted by
// paideia-as as movabs (10-byte imm64 form), legal for mov but NEVER
// legal in cmp/and. Callers gate error detection via
// `cmp rax, POSTUI_ERR_BAND_BASE` staged through a scratch register (or
// via the `cmp rax, 1; ja error` shortcut against the OK/error split
// since both sentinels are >> 1).
//
// =============================================================================
// UTF-8 CURSOR DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// text_input_cursor_left walks the byte at cursor_byte - 1 back through
// contiguous UTF-8 continuation bytes (0x80..0xBF) until it lands on a
// lead byte or a lone ASCII byte (< 0x80) -- so the cursor NEVER stops
// mid-codepoint. If cursor_byte == 0 on entry, the function is a no-op
// (returns 0 without touching the spec).
//
// text_input_cursor_right reads the byte at cursor_byte, dispatches on
// its lead-byte class:
//
//   b < 0x80      -> advance 1 (ASCII, complete one-byte codepoint)
//   b < 0xC0      -> advance 1 (stray continuation; defensive -- a
//                    well-formed cursor never lands here)
//   b < 0xE0      -> advance 2 (2-byte lead)
//   b < 0xF0      -> advance 3 (3-byte lead)
//   b < 0xF8      -> advance 4 (4-byte lead)
//   else          -> advance 1 (invalid lead 0xF8+, U+FFFD substitute)
//
// The result is clamped against buffer_len so a truncated multi-byte
// sequence at the tail (a 3-byte lead with only one byte remaining, for
// instance) still leaves the cursor at buffer_len rather than past it.
// If cursor_byte >= buffer_len on entry, the function is a no-op.
//
// text_input_insert and text_input_delete are BYTE-level operations by
// design (the M4-001 issue text explicitly frames them as "insert one
// byte" / "delete byte before cursor"). Well-formed UTF-8 sequence
// preservation is the caller's responsibility -- an editor stack that
// wants codepoint-atomic edits assembles them from insert() sequences
// for the encoded bytes of a codepoint and delete() sequences that walk
// back over continuation bytes first. This shape keeps the M4-001
// landing register-local and total, matching the byte-scan discipline
// Width and Text already use.
//
// =============================================================================
// DRAW ALGORITHM (text_input_draw)
// =============================================================================
//
// The draw body branches once on `mode`, running one of two body shapes.
// Both share the same prologue (arg cache, rect extract, early exits)
// and the same cell-write shape (word0 = byte | style<<32-ish laid via
// paragraph_draw's two-store pattern: mov_d [dst+0], byte_zx and
// mov [dst+4], style_masked -- byte lives in symbol slot's low byte,
// upper 3 zero; style word covers fg/bg/mods; cell bytes 12..15 retain
// their zeroed initial state from buffer_init).
//
// Both modes overlay the cursor cell in-place (not as a separate pass)
// by choosing style_cursor over style_normal whenever the byte's
// absolute buffer offset equals cursor_byte. A separate "cursor at end"
// post-pass handles cursor_byte == buffer_len (no byte to overlay --
// stamp an empty space cell with style_cursor).
//
// SingleLine mode (ti_dr_sl_):
//   for c in [0, bw):
//     abs = scroll_col + c
//     if abs < buffer_len:
//       byte  = buffer[abs]
//       style = (abs == cursor_byte) ? style_cursor : style_normal
//       stamp byte at (bx+c, by) with style
//     else if c == cursor_byte - scroll_col AND cursor_byte == buffer_len:
//       stamp space (0x20) at (bx+c, by) with style_cursor
//     (else: leave cell as buffer_init already set it)
//
// MultiLine mode (ti_dr_ml_):
//   Build a synthetic 24-B Span on stack: (ptr = buffer_ptr,
//     byte_len = buffer_len, style = 0). Style is 0 in the synthetic
//     Span because line_break_positions does not consult the Span
//     style word (it only touches ptr / byte_len).
//   Build a synthetic 24-B Line on stack: (spans_ptr = &span_slot,
//     span_count = 1, alignment = 0).
//   call line_break_positions(&line_slot, bw, break_array, 64) into a
//     64-slot u64 array at [rsp+0..512). A return > 64 (error sentinel
//     or over-cap) degrades to break_count = 0 -- the whole buffer is
//     rendered as one visual row truncated at the right edge, matching
//     paragraph_draw's own degrade posture (postui.M2-004).
//   for seg_idx in [0, break_count + 1) while cur_y < ye:
//     seg_end = (seg_idx < break_count) ? break[seg_idx] : buffer_len
//     for byte_off in [seg_start, seg_end):
//       col = byte_off - seg_start
//       if col >= bw: break (right-edge truncation)
//       byte  = buffer[byte_off]
//       style = (byte_off == cursor_byte) ? style_cursor : style_normal
//       stamp byte at (bx+col, cur_y) with style
//     seg_start = seg_end
//     cur_y += 1
//   Post-pass (cursor at end):
//     if cursor_byte == buffer_len:
//       Find last segment: cursor_row = by + break_count,
//                          cursor_col = buffer_len - (break_count == 0
//                                                    ? 0
//                                                    : break[break_count-1])
//       if cursor_row < ye AND cursor_col < bw:
//         stamp space (0x20) at (bx+cursor_col, cursor_row) with
//         style_cursor
//
// Both modes leave every cell OUTSIDE the write set untouched -- the
// caller's Frame::init zeroed the whole back region at bind time, so
// unwritten cells render as space with default style (matching every
// other postui widget's contract).
//
// v1 approximation: byte-per-cell for cell address computation
// (matches paragraph_draw, list_draw, table_draw). A CJK codepoint's
// 3-byte lead takes 3 cells rather than the 2 cells its display width
// would call for; a combining mark takes 1 cell rather than 0. A v2
// revision folding grapheme-cluster-aware address math lifts this to a
// per-cell Width::char_display_width dispatch; the cell-write shape
// stays byte-driven so the v2 change is localized to the address
// arithmetic in the inner loop, not the outer segment structure.
//
// =============================================================================
// PAIDEIA-AS CONFORMANCE
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`TextInput`), matches file
//     basename `text_input.pdx` per manifest.pdxproj snake_case ->
//     PascalCase mapping (same rule SemanticRegistry follows at
//     src/semantic_registry.pdx).
//   - No `test` mnemonic anywhere; every zero-check is `cmp reg, 0`.
//   - Every mov_b byte-load is preceded by `xor reg, reg` (paideia-as
//     pitfall #1248 load-side zero-extension). The mov_b byte-STORES
//     (used in insert/delete's shift loops and the buffer[cursor] write)
//     are unaffected by that pitfall; only load-side needs the pre-zero.
//   - All immediates in cmp/and fit imm32: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 0x20, 0x80,
//     0xC0, 0xE0, 0xF0, 0xF8, 0xFFFF, 64 -- widest is 0xFFFF = 65535 for
//     rect-field masks. Every shift count (3, 4, 8, 16, 32, 48) fits
//     imm8.
//   - The three sentinels (TI_ERR_BUFFER_FULL 0xFFFFEBE0,
//     TI_ERR_CURSOR_AT_START 0xFFFFEBE1, and TIVW_MAGIC 0x57564954)
//     are staged as follows: the two error sentinels via `mov reg,
//     imm64` (movabs; imm > 0x7FFFFFFF, legal for mov not cmp/and);
//     TIVW_MAGIC via `mov r32, imm32` (compact 5-byte form; imm <
//     0x80000000). No sentinel ever appears in cmp/and.
//   - No 2-op `imul reg, imm`. Every multiply is 2-op `imul reg, reg`
//     with the constant staged into a scratch register first (used
//     twice: for the cell address's y*cols product, and for the span-
//     count-times-24-byte stride during the synthetic-line setup;
//     actually the synthetic line uses span_count = 1 so the stride
//     step is unnecessary -- the synthetic path only needs one imul
//     for the address math).
//   - No `and reg, imm64`. Widest mask is 0xFFFF.
//   - Single-line `pub let X : type = value` for every constant.
//   - Module `TextInput = structure`, module keyword on the header line.
//   - r11 discipline: text_input_draw uses r11 as the per-iter dst
//     address (paragraph_draw / scrollbar_draw precedent). There is NO
//     `lea` in this module (unlike char_display_width, which loads a
//     rip-relative table), so paideia-as's r11 LEA-emission scratch
//     never touches these registers mid-use.
//   - SysV push/pop parity:
//       * text_input_spec_init is a pure leaf (no push/pop, no callee-
//         save writes, no nested call). Nine 8-byte stores.
//       * text_input_insert and text_input_delete are pure leafs (no
//         push/pop; the shift loops run in caller-save regs).
//       * text_input_cursor_left / cursor_right are pure leafs (no
//         push/pop; ASCII-fast-path + continuation-scan in caller-save
//         regs).
//       * text_input_draw pushes 5 callee-save regs (rbx / r12 / r13 /
//         r14 / r15 = 40 B; entry rsp%16==8, +40==0) plus `sub rsp,
//         736` for the stack scratch area (64-slot break array + span
//         slot + line slot + 25 state slots; 736 % 16 == 0 so rsp
//         stays aligned for the nested line_break_positions call).
//         One nested call site (line_break_positions) which the aligned
//         rsp satisfies without a further `sub rsp, 8`.
//       * text_input_view_pack is a pure leaf (no push/pop; four u64
//         copies + two u32 header stores).
//   - Cross-module call convention: bare `call line_break_positions`
//     per paragraph.pdx precedent (postui.M2-004). paideia-as's flat
//     symbol namespace resolves it against src/text.pdx's exported
//     symbol without a module::function prefix.
//   - Labels prefixed `ti_` per docs/design.md fingerprint discipline.
//     Sub-prefixes: ti_si_ (spec_init), ti_in_ (insert), ti_dl_
//     (delete), ti_cl_ (cursor_left), ti_cr_ (cursor_right), ti_dr_
//     (draw), ti_vp_ (view_pack). Every prefix scanned against the
//     union of existing prefixes (bl_ / bl_bv_ / rc_ / ri_ / cd_ / sd_
//     / cell_eq_ / cl_ / ci_ / wk_ / sp_ / sw_ / ln_ / lw_ / tx_ /
//     tw_ / bp_ / pg_ / pg_pv_ / ls_ / tb_ / tab_ / sc_ / sr_ / ga_ /
//     sk_ / bc_ / ch_ / cvs_) confirms no collision.
//   - Effect row `!{mem}` on every function -- reads through spec_ptr /
//     buffer_ptr, writes through buffer_ptr / buf_ptr / out_ptr.

module TextInput = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // TextInputSpec layout (72 bytes)
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TI_SPEC_BYTES        : u64 = 72

  pub let TI_OFF_RECT          : u64 = 0
  pub let TI_OFF_BUFFER_PTR    : u64 = 8
  pub let TI_OFF_BUFFER_CAP    : u64 = 16
  pub let TI_OFF_BUFFER_LEN    : u64 = 24
  pub let TI_OFF_CURSOR_BYTE   : u64 = 32
  pub let TI_OFF_SCROLL_COL    : u64 = 40
  pub let TI_OFF_MODE          : u64 = 48
  pub let TI_OFF_STYLE_NORMAL  : u64 = 56
  pub let TI_OFF_STYLE_CURSOR  : u64 = 64

  // ==========================================================================
  // Mode ordinals (docs/design.md §2.6 TextInput vs TextArea distinction).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TI_MODE_SINGLE : u64 = 0
  pub let TI_MODE_MULTI  : u64 = 1

  // ==========================================================================
  // Wrap scratch cap. text_input_draw allocates a 64-slot u64 break-
  // position array on its own stack (64 * 8 = 512 B) and passes it as
  // line_break_positions' out buffer. A return > 64 (either the
  // TEXT_ERR_BUF_TOO_SMALL sentinel 0xFFFFEBCA or an over-cap count)
  // is read as "wrap needed more breaks than the cap allows"; the
  // draw degrades to break_count = 0 (whole buffer renders as one
  // visual row, right-edge-truncated). 64 breaks covers a 4096-cell
  // buffer at a 64-column wrap width -- matches paragraph.pdx L334.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TI_BREAKS_MAX : u64 = 64

  // ==========================================================================
  // Error sentinels. Anchored at Lib::POSTUI_ERR_BAND_BASE (0xFFFFEB80)
  // + 0x60 offset = 0xFFFFEBE0 (M4 batch band start; see ERROR BAND
  // block in the module header). Both values > 0x7FFFFFFF -- movabs
  // required for `mov reg, imm`; NEVER usable in cmp/and.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TI_ERR_BAND            : u64 = 0xFFFFEBE0
  pub let TI_ERR_BUFFER_FULL     : u64 = 0xFFFFEBE0
  pub let TI_ERR_CURSOR_AT_START : u64 = 0xFFFFEBE1

  // ==========================================================================
  // TextInputView@0.1 semantic-pipe wire (docs/design.md §2.3).
  //
  // TIVW_MAGIC = 0x57564954 -- little-endian bytes 0x54 0x49 0x56 0x57
  // spell "TIVW" (TextInput View). Fits imm32 (< 0x7FFFFFFF) so the
  // packer's magic store uses the compact 5-byte `mov r32, imm32`
  // form, not a 10-byte movabs.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let TIVW_MAGIC           : u64 = 0x57564954
  pub let TIVW_RECORD_BYTES    : u64 = 40
  pub let TIVW_OFF_MAGIC       : u64 = 0
  pub let TIVW_OFF_RECORD_LEN  : u64 = 4
  pub let TIVW_OFF_RECT        : u64 = 8
  pub let TIVW_OFF_CURSOR_BYTE : u64 = 16
  pub let TIVW_OFF_BUFFER_LEN  : u64 = 24
  pub let TIVW_OFF_MODE        : u64 = 32

  // Schema name ("TextInputView@0.1", NUL-terminated). Kept here as the
  // authoritative source so the M2-009 BLAKE3-hash landing (if it
  // upgrades TIVW's schema constant beyond the u32 MAGIC) has exactly
  // one string to hash. Byte length 17 + 1 terminator = 18.
  pub let TIVW_SCHEMA_NAME     : [u8; 18] = "TextInputView@0.1\0"
  pub let TIVW_SCHEMA_NAME_LEN : u64 = 17

  // ==========================================================================
  // text_input_spec_init(dst, rect, buffer_ptr, buffer_cap,
  //                       buffer_len_and_cursor, scroll_and_mode, styles)
  //                       -> ()
  //
  // Pack a TextInputSpec into 72 bytes at [dst..dst+72). Ten u64 fields
  // do not fit in the 6-arg SysV register budget (rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9),
  // so three of the fields are folded into two composite arguments:
  //
  //   buffer_len_and_cursor bits:
  //     [ 0..32)  buffer_len   (u32 truncation; caller responsibility to
  //                             keep len <= cap and both < 4G; a spec
  //                             for a > 4G buffer already lives outside
  //                             the design-scope taxonomy)
  //     [32..64)  cursor_byte  (u32 truncation)
  //
  //   scroll_and_mode bits:
  //     [ 0..32)  scroll_col   (u32 truncation)
  //     [32..64)  mode         (u32 truncation; only 0/1 are valid at v1)
  //
  //   styles is a u64 pair passed via the stack (7th SysV arg would
  //   spill to [rsp+8] anyway). To keep the ABI compact, we split it
  //   as follows: instead of taking BOTH style words as separate args,
  //   we take a single `styles_packed` word that carries style_normal
  //   in low 32 bits and style_cursor in high 32 bits -- but Style
  //   is a 64-bit wire (fg + bg + mods = 56 bits, fits u56). A u32-
  //   truncated Style would lose the top mods bit and half the bg
  //   channels. So we instead accept the two Style words as separate
  //   args, meaning this function DOES need 7 args: dst, rect,
  //   buffer_ptr, buffer_cap, buffer_len_and_cursor, scroll_and_mode,
  //   style_normal, style_cursor. That is 8 args, exceeding SysV.
  //
  // Resolution: accept 6 args fully in registers, fold the two Style
  // words into a single styles-slot the caller stages ADJACENT in the
  // spec after the register phase. That is, this function takes 6
  // args as above but writes styles as 0/0; callers that need per-cell
  // styling patch [dst+56] and [dst+64] with the two Style words
  // directly after this returns.
  //
  // A cleaner alternative -- ship a companion text_input_spec_init_styles
  // helper for the two style fields -- keeps the two-argument budget
  // and lets a caller drop the styles set entirely if they are happy
  // with default (0) styles. This landing ships both helpers.
  //
  // Register plan (spec_init proper): rdi=dst, rsi=rect,
  // rdx=buffer_ptr, rcx=buffer_cap, r8=buffer_len_and_cursor,
  // r9=scroll_and_mode, rax=extract scratch. Pure leaf: no callee-save
  // touched, no push/pop, no nested call. Effect `!{mem}` (nine 8-byte
  // stores).
  //
  // Labels prefixed ti_si_ per docs fingerprint discipline (none needed
  // -- straight-line body).
  // ==========================================================================
